# Hivemind Expert System

Expert agents are managed centrally via the `hivemind` CLI.

## Commands

```
hivemind expert list              # See all experts and their status
hivemind expert show <name>       # Show expert details
hivemind expert add <url>         # Register, clone, AI-analyze, and create expert agent
hivemind expert enable <name>     # Enable an expert (clones repo + deploys agent)
hivemind expert disable <name>    # Disable an expert (removes agent)
hivemind expert delete <name>     # Delete an expert entirely
hivemind expert update [name]     # Fetch latest commits and re-analyze with AI
hivemind expert query <question>  # Ask the librarian which expert(s) can help
hivemind team list                # List all teams
hivemind team create <name> -d <desc> -e <expert1,expert2,...>  # Create team with experts
hivemind team show <name>         # Show team details and roster
hivemind team add-expert <t> <e>  # Add an expert to a team
hivemind team remove-expert <t> <e> # Remove an expert from a team
hivemind team delete <name>       # Delete a team
hivemind status                   # Full dashboard
hivemind redeploy                 # Regenerate all agent files
hivemind init                     # Set up provider directory structure
```

## Architecture

- Deployed agents: `agents/` — subagent definitions with provider-specific frontmatter
- Librarian: `agents/librarian.md` — auto-generated catalog of all experts and teams
- Expert knowledge: `experts/<name>/` — versioned knowledge docs and cloned repo source
- Team notes: `{{ teams_path }}/<team>/` — team lead notes, per-expert consultation journals
- Fetched repos: `~/.cache/hivemind/repos/<name>`

## Workflow

You (main) handle all implementation directly. Subagents are for domain research only — they cannot spawn other agents.

### When you need domain expertise:

1. **Consult the team lead** (first time or when unsure) — spawn `team-lead-{team}` to get routing advice. The team lead knows each expert's strengths and recent consultation history (via notes.md files). It will recommend which expert(s) to call and what to ask. On follow-up questions where you already know which expert to call, you can skip this step.

2. **Spawn experts** — when spawning each expert, you MUST include these instructions in your prompt every time, even on follow-ups:
   - The specific question to answer
   - **If consulting as part of a team:**
     - That they are being consulted as part of the `{team}` team
     - The path to their notes file: `{{ teams_path }}/{team}/expert-{name}/notes.md`
     - That they must update their notes file with: date, what was asked, and a summary of their findings
     - If the expert is on multiple teams, use the team that's relevant to the current task
   - **If consulting a solo expert (not in a team):** do NOT ask them to take notes. Solo consultations have no reader for notes and no durable home — they'd just pollute local state. Anything worth remembering long-term should be folded into the expert's knowledge docs via `hivemind update`.

3. **Experts update their notes (team consultations only)** — each team-scoped expert writes to `{{ teams_path }}/{team}/expert-{name}/notes.md` with what was asked and what they found. This builds institutional memory across sessions that the team lead reads back when routing. If a team expert does not update its notes, remind it to do so. Solo experts do not take notes.

### When you don't know which team or expert to use:

Spawn the **librarian** (`librarian`) — it knows every expert and team and will recommend who to consult.

### Key principles:

- **You do the implementation** — read code, write code, run commands. Subagents research and advise.
- **Subagents cannot spawn other agents** — only you can. Depth is limited to 1.
- **Maximize parallel expert calls** — if you need input from multiple experts, spawn them all at once.
- **Experts are grounded in source code** — they have access to cloned repos and structured knowledge docs. Trust their findings over general knowledge.
- **Notes are team-scoped** — only team-scoped consultations produce notes. Solo expert consultations do not. Notes exist so the team lead can read them back for routing; without a team, there's no reader.
